  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nQuiet weather continues through this evening before shower \nchances gradually increase late tonight into Wednesday as the \nnext wave and associated boundary approach from the west. \nPrecipitation will become more widespread Wednesday into \nThursday as the system tracks through the region. Overall \nrainfall rates should remain on the lighter side, as relatively \ndry low and mid levels will limit precipitation efficiency, with\nrain spread out over a prolonged period of time. Northern \nLower, particularly areas south of M-32, look favored for higher\nrainfall totals, with around 0.50\" to 0.85\" expected late \ntonight through Thursday night with higher amounts possible the \nfarther south you go, including around an inch or more across \nportions of far southern northern Lower (around and south of \nM-55)... Again, these are storm total rainfall amounts over the \ncourse of 36 hours. Amounts should generally taper the farther \nnorth one goes. \n\nThe midweek wave gradually departs Thursday night into Friday, \nwith a few lingering low-end shower chances possible Friday \nbefore high pressure builds back in. Temperatures will cool \nbehind the system, with highs settling into the 60s and \novernight lows generally in the 40s or cooler through the \nweekend. Overall, a much quieter and drier pattern is expected \nFriday into early next week as high pressure influences the \nsensible weather, with ample dry time expected through the \nweekend. Shower chances still linger Sunday as some guidance \ncontinues to suggest a weak disturbance could clip the area, but\nconfidence remains low with this being several days out. \nOtherwise, seasonably cool temperatures for early October and \ngenerally dry conditions look to continue into Tuesday.",
